This enables computing expressions over the abstract domain of intervals. If the result of an computation is a singleton then we can safely replace it with its contents. This has the benefit of being kinda of a constant propagation and a smarter simplifier. 

There is the interesting bit that this can be "tuned": it can be fast/imprecise or slow/precise by playing with the interval analysis flags. For example:

```c
int main() {
  int a = 1;
  int b = 2;
  int c = a + b;

  for(int i = 0; i < b; i++);
  return 0;  
}
```

With just plain interval analysis will become:

```c
int main() {
  int a = 1;
  int b = 2;
  int c = 3;

  for(int i = 0; i < c; i++);
  return 0;  
}
```

While interval analysis and interval arithmetic will become:

```c
int main() {
  int a = 1;
  int b = 2;
  int c = 3;

  for(int i = 0; i < 3; i++);
  return 0;  
}
```

~Note: This is still a draft because there is a bug in the arithmetic that I need to fix before this can be merged. Right now we need to also activate interval extrapolation to get any results with arithmetic.~

